  Who are stakeholders?
   What are their needs?
   What viewports we will use and what we will show, what stakeholder needs we will satisfy?




How to Use Viewpoints Effectively ✅

When creating a view (even without a formal viewpoint), always ask:

    What concerns does this address? (Performance? Security?)

    What elements does it show? (Servers? Code modules?)

    Who’s the audience? (Devs? Execs? Users?)

📢 Pro Tip: Document your viewpoint rules so everyone’s on the same page!




1. Context Viewpoint (The Big Picture)

    What? Shows how your system interacts with the world

    Why? Aligns everyone on scope and boundaries

    Key Question: "Who/what interacts with our system and how?"

2. Functional Viewpoint (The Blueprint)

    What? The system's components and their interactions

    Why? Foundation for all other technical decisions

    Pro Tip: This is often the first view stakeholders want to see

3. Information Viewpoint (The Data Lens)

    What? How data flows and transforms through the system

    Why? Data is usually the whole point of the system!

    Key Question: "Where does data live and how does it move?"

4. Concurrency Viewpoint (The Performance Optimizer)

    What? Parallel processing and threading models

    Why? Critical for performance and scalability

    Watch For: Race conditions and deadlock risks

The Supporting Cast
5. Development Viewpoint (The Builder's Guide)

    What? Code structure, frameworks, and standards

    Why? Ensures maintainable implementation

    Pro Tip: Great for onboarding new developers

6. Deployment Viewpoint (The Production Map)

    What? Hardware, networking, and environment needs

    Why? Bridges the gap between code and reality

    Key Question: "Where will this actually run?"

7. Operational Viewpoint (The Runbook)

    What? How to keep the system alive in production

    Why? Often overlooked until it's too late!

    Must Have: Disaster recovery and monitoring plans




Pro Tips for Implementation

    Start with Context - Get alignment before diving deep

    Functional view is your anchor - Other views build on it

    Don't over-document - Focus on what matters for your system

    Validate across views - Check for consistency regularly




Security isn’t alone here. Performance, scalability, resilience? These cross-cutting concerns impact all views, not just one.

💡 Key Takeaway: Instead of forcing quality properties into isolated viewpoints, bake them into each relevant view—because great architecture isn’t just about features; it’s about how the whole system holds up under real-world demands.




💡 Why Perspectives Matter

They’re your cheat sheet for:
✅ Avoiding blind spots – Catch flaws early (e.g., a "perfect" design that’s unusably slow).
✅ Guiding new architects – Learn best practices fast.
✅ Standardizing reviews – Checklists ensure nothing slips through.
📐 How to Use a Perspective (Structured for Success!)

Every perspective follows the same proven template:

    🎯 Applicability
    Which views does this perspective impact most?
    (e.g., Evolution → Functional View; Performance → Deployment View)

    ⚠️ Concerns
    What quality properties are at stake?
    (e.g., Security = confidentiality, integrity, availability)

    🛠️ Activities
    Step-by-step: How to apply the perspective.

        Assess current design.

        Identify gaps.

        Implement fixes (using tactics).

    ⚔️ Architectural Tactics
    Proven strategies to hit quality goals.

        Security: Encryption, rate limiting, zero-trust.

        Performance: Caching, async processing, CDNs.

    🚨 Problems & Pitfalls
    Classic mistakes (and how to dodge them).

        Example: "Over-encrypting = terrible latency."

    ✔️ Checklists
    Quick validations:

        "Is auth data hashed?"

        "Are retries handled for transient failures?"

    📚 Further Reading
    Deep dives for when you need more.




    	Security 🔐	Performance ⚡	Evolution 🔄
Functional	Auth flows, role-based access	Optimized APIs, caching	Modular design for future features
Information	Encryption, data ownership	Database indexing, query tuning	Schema versioning
Concurrency	Rate limiting, thread safety	Lock granularity, async processing	Stateless services
Deployment	Firewalls, zero-trust	Load balancing, auto-scaling	Immutable infrastructure

(Example: Applying Security to the Information View = Encrypted data + strict access controls.)
🛠️ Key Steps to Apply Perspectives

    Prioritize

        Not all combos are equally important! Focus on high-risk intersections first (e.g., Security + Deployment for a public cloud app).

        Use a traffic-light system (🔴 High priority, 🟡 Medium, 🟢 Low) in your grid.

    Analyze & Adapt

        For each relevant cell:

            Assess: Does the current design meet quality goals?

            Tactics: Apply proven fixes (e.g., Caching for Performance + Functional View).

            Validate: Test assumptions (e.g., load-test scaled APIs).

    Document Decisions

        Capture trade-offs: "Chose eventual consistency (over strong consistency) to boost Availability in the Information View."




